n}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 7+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{\\sqrt{38}\\sqrt{76}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{38\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 7+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{\\sqrt{38}\\sqrt{76}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{38\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186351471 :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- **II.** $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- **III.** $T$ is a linear transformation\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- **II.** $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- **III.** $T$ is a linear transformation\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186351471 :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5(kx)+2(ky) \\\\ 3(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(5x+2y) \\\\ k(3y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}=kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\  & T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5(kx)+2(ky) \\\\ 3(ky)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}k(5x+2y) \\\\ k(3y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{1}+2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186351472 :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- **II.** $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- **III.** $T$ is a linear transformation\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- **II.** $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- **III.** $T$ is a linear transformation\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186351472 :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3ky \\\\ 2kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}=kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\  & T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3ky \\\\ 2kx\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186351473 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal."] [:db/cas 17592186351473 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})≈{28.13}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-\\frac{1}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})≈{28.13}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186351474 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal."] [:db/cas 17592186351474 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186351475 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5y-12x \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5y-12x \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351475 :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351499 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix},$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351500 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351501 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351513 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it is never equal to zero. So, $f$ has no critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary: Notice that $D$ is the square with vertices at $(-1,-1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(1,1),$  and $(-1,1)$ \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,-1) \\\\  & =(t)-(-1)+1 \\\\  & =t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =(1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =2-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,1) \\\\  & =(t)-(1)+1 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAnd finally, \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(-1,t) \\\\  & =(-1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(-1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3$ at the point $(1,-1).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}1,\\,-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it is never equal to zero. So, $f$ has no critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary: Notice that $D$ is the square with vertices at $(-1,-1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(1,1),$  and $(-1,1)$ \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,-1) \\\\  & =(t)-(-1)+1 \\\\  & =t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =(1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =2-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,1) \\\\  & =(t)-(1)+1 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAnd finally, \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(-1,t) \\\\  & =(-1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(-1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3$ at the point $(1,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186351518 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351518 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351522 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351522 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(3-0)^{2}+(5-5)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+0+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\hat{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(3-0)^{2}+(5-5)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+0+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351525 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351525 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 12 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}65 \\\\ -68\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{19}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}65 \\\\ -68\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-21 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 12 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}65 \\\\ -68\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{19}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}65 \\\\ -68\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351532 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351532 :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2.$" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351533 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186351533 :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351534 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351534 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351535 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n- **U.** $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **V.** $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **W.** $W={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n- **U.** $U={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **V.** $V={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **W.** $W={\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186351535 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=12+0+1=13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]=-40+40=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=-1+0+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=12+0+1=13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}=-40+40=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186351542 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **V.** $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **W.** $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **V.** $V={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **W.** $W={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186351542 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=0+1+8=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=0+1+8=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186351544 :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351544 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-3{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-3{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351545 :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351545 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-4(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4})+2{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=3$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-4(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4})+2{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=3$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351549 :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351549 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{5}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt