egin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354492 :question/problem "Find ${A}^{71},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find ${A}^{71},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354492 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354493 :question/problem "Find ${M}^{24},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" "Find ${M}^{24},$ where\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354493 :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{24} & =P{D}^{24}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{24}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[12]{2})^{24} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[8]{2})^{24}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 40 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{24} & =P{D}^{24}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{24}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[12]{2})^{24} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[8]{2})^{24}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 40 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354494 :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}E=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354494 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186354495 :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354495 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$" "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186354496 :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]T=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}T=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354496 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$" "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186354506 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$" "Let $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$"] [:db/cas 17592186354506 :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{3+2+(-2)+12}{1+4+1+9}a \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{15}a \\\\  & =a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{3+2+(-2)+12}{1+4+1+9}a \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{15}a \\\\  & =a \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186354507 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186354507 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354508 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354508 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}14 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 23\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{45}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 23\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354515 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354515 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-1)^{2}+(0-(-1))^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\hat{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-1)^{2}+(0-(-1))^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354518 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186354518 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{3\\cdot 8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{3})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{3\\cdot 8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{3})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186354522 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354522 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354523 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186354523 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $b=5,$ $c=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-2}{5}=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $b=5,$ $c=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-2}{5}=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354524 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186354524 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 5+\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{5}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{5}{2})}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 5+\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{5}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{5}{2})}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354525 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186354525 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=-\\frac{13}{3},$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-1}{(-\\frac{13}{3})}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=-\\frac{13}{3},$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-1}{(-\\frac{13}{3})}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354526 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186354526 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5},$ $c=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}|+|\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\