3 & -4 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -12 & -24 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -16 & -11 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-3{C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -12 & -24 & -13 & 5 \\\\ -16 & -11 & -17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-5{C}_{4}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-3{C}_{4}.$" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-4{C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -12 & 1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -16 & 9 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-5{C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -12 & -24 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -16 & -11 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-3{C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -12 & -24 & -13 & 5 \\\\ -16 & -11 & -17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-5{C}_{4}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-3{C}_{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357511 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\n- ${C}_{1}:=…$\n- ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}$\n- ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\n- ${C}_{1}:=…$\n- ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}$\n- ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?"] [:db/cas 17592186357511 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-7{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-7{C}_{3}.$" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-7{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-7{C}_{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357521 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(1,0)$\n- **II.** $(-1,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(1,0)$\n- **II.** $(-1,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,2)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357521 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357522 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(14,6),$ and $(9,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(3,2)$\n- **II.** $(1,1)$\n- **III.** $(5,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(14,6),$ and $(9,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(3,2)$\n- **II.** $(1,1)$\n- **III.** $(5,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357522 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357523 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(4,-1),(17,-3),$ and $(10,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(3,2)$\n- **II.** $(-1,1)$\n- **III.** $(5,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(4,-1),(17,-3),$ and $(10,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(3,2)$\n- **II.** $(-1,1)$\n- **III.** $(5,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357523 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-0}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357524 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-21,0),(21,0),$ and $(0,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(0,-3)$\n- **II.** $(3,1)$\n- **III.** $(-3,3)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-21,0),(21,0),$ and $(0,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n- **I.** $(0,-3)$\n- **II.** $(3,1)$\n- **III.** $(-3,3)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357524 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-7-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-3,0),(3,0),(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-7-0}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-3,0),(3,0),(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357525 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,2),(-5,4),(-3,2),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(-2,1)$\n- **II.** $(0,0)$\n- **III.** $(2,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,2),(-5,4),(-3,2),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(-2,1)$\n- **II.** $(0,0)$\n- **III.** $(2,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357525 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3-2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2),(-1,2),(-1,0),(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3-2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2),(-1,2),(-1,0),(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357526 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(1,3)$\n- **II.** $(3,3)$\n- **III.** $(2,3)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(1,3)$\n- **II.** $(3,3)$\n- **III.** $(2,3)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357526 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357527 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(0,-1)$\n- **II.** $(1,1)$\n- **III.** $(-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(0,-1)$\n- **II.** $(1,1)$\n- **III.** $(-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357527 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357528 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,0),(-2,-2),(-4,0),$ and $(-2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(2,2)$\n- **II.** $(0,1)$\n- **III.** $(-2,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,0),(-2,-2),(-4,0),$ and $(-2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n- **I.** $(2,2)$\n- **II.** $(0,1)$\n- **III.** $(-2,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357528 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,0),(2,0),(2,2),(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,0),(2,0),(2,2),(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357534 :question/problem "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357542 :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd50e99a129b1d6a2c6b73145dbf68681bc92a3cef0ca8e743b63cf96c9afafc.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "![](images/bd/bd50e99a129b1d6a2c6b73145dbf68681bc92a3cef0ca8e743b63cf96c9afafc.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357544 :question/problem "![](images/24/24eadcb5300028866834aec17b105794078a17606085191315475a9aae3be5ef.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "![](images/24/24eadcb5300028866834aec17b105794078a17606085191315475a9aae3be5ef.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357549 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357549 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-7\\cdot 7 & =0 \\\\ 4-2\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 45}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{196}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 14}{2} \\\\  & =-5,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =9.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-7\\cdot 7 & =0 \\\\ 4-2\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 45}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{196}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 14}{2} \\\\  & =-5,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357550 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$L=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$L=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357550 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $L-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nL-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(L-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(L-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-5\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ -2+\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 7}{2} \\\\  & =-3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $L-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nL-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(L-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(L-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-5\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ -2+\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 7}{2} \\\\  & =-3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357565 :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357565 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =8.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-12,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-12}{-2}=6.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =8.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-12,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-12}{-2}=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357567 :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357567 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357576 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357576 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357584 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357584 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357595 :question/problem "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$   Evaluate the function when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].$" "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$   Evaluate the function when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357595 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =12-8 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =12-8 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357600 :question/problem "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357600 :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3+3-2-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3+3-2-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357603 :question/problem "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$  Evaluate the function when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$" "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  Evaluate the function when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357603 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 8+1\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =18-8-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 8+1\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =18-8-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357604 :question/problem "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}].$  Evaluate the function when $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$  Evaluate the function when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357604 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 6+(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-20+18+8 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 6+(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-20+18+8 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357605 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357605 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357606 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,-2 & 4 \\\\ \\,2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,-2 & 4 \\\\ \\,2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357606 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357607 :question/problem "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)=2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)=2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357607 :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0-24-1-0 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0-24-1-0 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357608 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357608 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357609 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357609 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+1{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+1{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357610 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357610 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357611 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357611 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357612 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357612 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\"" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186357613 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357613 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 