e the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\ -{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357615 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357615 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357616 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357616 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."] [:db/cas 17592186357617 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357617 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."] [:db/cas 17592186357620 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357620 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."] [:db/cas 17592186357621 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{13} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{5}{13} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{12}{13} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{5}{13} & \\,∗\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357621 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."] [:db/cas 17592186357630 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357630 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357631 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357631 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357632 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)=-4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)=-4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357632 :question/worked-solution "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =-4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-4){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+\\,\\,3{x}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-5){x}_{2}{y}_{1}\\,+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-4$ and $b=3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=-4+3=-1.\n$$" "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =-4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-4){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+\\,\\,3{x}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-5){x}_{2}{y}_{1}\\,+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-4$ and $b=3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=-4+3=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357633 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & a \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)=6{x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & a \\\\ 0 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)=6{x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357633 :question/worked-solution "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =6{x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =6{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}\\,+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=(-2)+3=1.\n$$" "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =6{x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =6{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}\\,+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=(-2)+3=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357634 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ a & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a-b?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ a & \\,\\,-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a-b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357634 :question/worked-solution "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}\\,\\,+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=b=2.$ Finally,\n\n$$\na-b=2-2=0.\n$$" "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}\\,\\,+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=b=2.$ Finally,\n\n$$\na-b=2-2=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357635 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357635 :question/worked-solution "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+(-3){x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+(-3)=0.\n$$" "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+(-3){x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+(-3)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357636 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357636 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357637 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357637 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357638 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & a & 0 \\\\ b & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & a & 0 \\\\ b & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357638 :question/worked-solution "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =0{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+(-3){x}_{3}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=3,$ and $c=2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+3+2=6.\n$$" "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =0{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+(-3){x}_{3}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=3,$ and $c=2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+3+2=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357639 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,L=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,M=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357640 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357641 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{4}?$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{4}?$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,F=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186357643 :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357643 :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}+6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4+2+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}+6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4+2+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357644 :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357644 :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)+(-2)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0+4+0-0+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)+(-2)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0+4+0-0+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357646 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357646 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357647 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357647 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 13\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357649 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357649 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357650 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357650 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =2{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(-4){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-4){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =2{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(-4){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-4){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357651 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357651 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =2{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(3){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(3){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =2{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(3){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(3){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357652 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357652 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+1{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+1{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3})+{x}_{2}({x}_{2})+{x}_{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+1{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+1{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3})+{x}_{2}({x}_{2})+{x}_{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357654 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357654 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =2{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+1{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+{x}_{3}(2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{x}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =2{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+1{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+{x}_{3}(2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{x}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357656 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357656 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+(-1){x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}(-{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+(-1){x}_{2}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+(-1){x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}(-{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357659 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $b+c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357659 :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+(-3){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-3){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{-6}{2}=-3\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-3$ and $c=1.$ Finally, \n\n$$\nb+c=-3+1=-2.\n$$" "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+(-3){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-3){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{-6}{2}=-3\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-3$ and $c=1.$ Finally, \n\n$$\nb+c=-3+1=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357660 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & b \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $b+c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & b \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357660 :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,1{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+(-2){x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{2}{2}=1\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=1$ and $c=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\nb+c=1-2=-1.\n$$" "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,1{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+(-2){x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{2}{2}=1\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=1$ and $c=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\nb+c=1-2=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357661 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-2{x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ b & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-2{x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357661 :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){x}_{1}^{2}+(-5){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-5){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+(-3){x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{-10}{2}=-5\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=-5.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=-2+(-5)=-7.\n$$" "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){x}_{1}^{2}+(-5){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-5){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+(-3){x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{-10}{2}=-5\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ and $b=-5.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=-2+(-5)=-7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357662 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & b \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ b & 0 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 & b \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ b & 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357662 :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-5){x}_{1}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,1{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}^{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,(-5){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{2}{2}=1,$ and\n\n- we split the coefficient of $-10{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{13}={a}_{31}=\\frac{-10}{2}=-5.$\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+(-5)+3=1.\n$$" "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-5){x}_{1}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,1{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}^{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,(-5){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{2}{2}=1,$ and\n\n- we split the coefficient of $-10{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{13}={a}_{31}=\\frac{-10}{2}=-5.$\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=3.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+(-5)+3=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357663 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & b \\\\ 6 & a & 0 \\\\ b & 0 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & b \\\\ 6 & a & 0 \\\\ b & 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186357663 :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,6{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of $12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{12}{2}=6,$ and\n\n- we split the coefficient of $-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{13}={a}_{31}=\\frac{-4}{2}=-2.$\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=0.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-2)+0=-1.\n$$" "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,6{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of $12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{12}{2}=6,$ and\n\n- we split the coefficient of $-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{13}={a}_{31}=\\frac{-4}{2}=-2.$\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=0.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-2)+0=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357664 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ in terms of the components of $y.$" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ in terms of the components of $y.$"]]
